#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175704 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32c0fb55-c598-521f-aab9-72efc6b70789" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Time Intervals on which a Particle is Moving in a Particular Direction"}] :question/id #uuid "d1b0ae55-f596-5aa9-ab2a-656b420207e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-564" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s^2,$ given by the function $a(t) = 3t^2+2t-6,$ where $t > 0$ is the time in seconds. The positive direction is left to right. If the particle is stationary when $t=0\\,\\textrm s,$ determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving to the right." :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}+2t-6)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}+{t}^{2}-6t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the particle is stationary at the time $t=0.$ This means $v(0) = 0,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(0)^{3}+(0)^{2}-6(0)+C \\\\ 0 & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore the velocity of the particle is\n\n$$\nv(t) = t^3+t^2-6t.\n$$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the particle is moving to the *right*, we must solve the inequality $v > 0.$ This gives\n\n$$\nt^3+t^2-6t = t(t+3)(t-2) > 0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t),$ we get the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/assets/03b5ff6282d59038cbb355c0c3f017b544f286228ba4c3a82329897b4b43674c.png)\n\nThe particle moves to the right when $v(t)$ is positive. So, from the graph, we see that the particle moves to the right when $t>2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175705 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0a81fe3-c8f1-58ec-ba93-116997b15f1c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Times at Which a Particle Reaches Its Maximum Velocity"}] :question/id #uuid "9860b59c-116e-5d87-a76a-5ca8062b8bb7" :question/math-academy-id "e-565" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s^2,$ given by the function $a(t) = 6t-3t^2,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s$ when $t=1\\,\\textrm s,$ calculate the maximum velocity $v_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity." :question/worked-solution "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d t} = a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t) = 0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 6t-3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3t(2-t) & =0 \\\\ t & =0,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t> 0$, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $t = 2\\,\\textrm s.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{″}(t) & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t-3{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =6-6t \\\\ {v}^{″}(2) & =6-6(2) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $v''(2) <      0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=2\\,\\textrm s.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (6t-3{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1) = 2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(1)^{2}-(1)^{3}+C & =2 \\\\ 3-1+C & =2\\,⟹\\,C=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t) =3t^2-t^3.$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\nv_{\\text{max}}= v(2) = 3(2)^2 - (2)^3 =4\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s\n$$\n\nSo the maximum velocity $v_{\\text{max}}=4\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s$ is reached at $t=2\\,\\textrm s.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175722 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c28b400d-9ec3-53c6-8e33-0133598ed143" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Times at Which a Particle is Stationary"}] :question/id #uuid "0bfe5f74-9c89-5dfb-9418-7c91793b5e1e" :question/math-academy-id "e-7226" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s^2,$ given by the function $a(t) =2t-6$, where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=4\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s$ when $t=1\\,\\textrm s$, determine the moments in time when the particle is stationary." :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2t-6)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}-6t+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1) =4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =(1)^{2}-6(1)+C \\\\ 4 & =-5+C \\\\ C & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t) = t^2-6t + 9.$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-6t+9 & =0 \\\\ (t-3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ t & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary when $t=3 \\,\\textrm s.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347359 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32c0fb55-c598-521f-aab9-72efc6b70789" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Time Intervals on which a Particle is Moving in a Particular Direction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c7132f7-047a-51cb-82fb-2281c5091c9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1<t<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347361} {:answer/id #uuid "67612c77-0a0f-5f8b-a6bc-9aa5913c6395" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<t<1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347362} {:answer/id #uuid "0dcd8556-a6c6-513e-b2be-72186509d693" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t>4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347363} {:answer/id #uuid "cd674330-ae61-565f-bb46-40b875c09a51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t>1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347364} {:answer/id #uuid "5acda7ef-40eb-5924-9298-ae379d4f1f67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<t<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c7132f7-047a-51cb-82fb-2281c5091c9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1<t<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347361} :answer-field/id #uuid "5605b0db-6bc6-5bf3-97a7-5406c5e813de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2be0b383-f913-54bf-8320-d57af23e10c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-120755" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ $a(t)=2t-5,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. The positive direction is left to right. If the particle has velocity $v=10\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=6\\,\\text{s},$ determine the time intervals in which the particle moves to the left." :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2t-5)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}-5t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(6)=10.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =(6)^{2}-5(6)+C \\\\ 10 & =6+C \\\\ C & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{2}-5t+4.$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the particle is moving to the *left*, we must solve the inequality $v<0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}-5t+4=(t-1)(t-4)<0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t)$, we get the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/assets/ad76f817f7c6e4a254e8881c34270eb875e72d821531566cb141cb393dcead72.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $v<0$ whenever $1<t<4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347366 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32c0fb55-c598-521f-aab9-72efc6b70789" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Time Intervals on which a Particle is Moving in a Particular Direction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "171625ef-8dc7-5b34-a8bd-0d3b7b06942c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<t<3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347368} {:answer/id #uuid "472a4780-c05a-552d-b4a2-219deabc49d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1<t<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347369} {:answer/id #uuid "ada0f5b2-db9a-5904-9297-12420b6cb2fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347370} {:answer/id #uuid "c1e2a6e0-73c1-5839-89a3-675aec1875ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t>4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347371} {:answer/id #uuid "02fc2ff1-db16-5b1a-ad1d-dbe65323d8e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<t<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02fc2ff1-db16-5b1a-ad1d-dbe65323d8e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<t<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347372} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd447f17-d0bd-57f8-8c74-29519d271329" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3000b364-333a-5918-a5a9-f4862710ed59" :question/math-academy-id "q-120757" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=3{t}^{2}-6t-4,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  The positive direction is left to right. If the particle is stationary when $t=4\\,\\text{s},$ determine the time intervals in which the particle moves to the left." :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}-6t-4)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-4t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(4)^{3}-3(4)^{2}-4(4)+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-4t.$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the particle is moving to the *left*, we must solve the inequality $v<0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-4t=t(t-4)(t+1)<0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t)$, we get the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/assets/002f547b882940d087d6ed9d228334ec163931cfea0665eafdfe8b6ec8d863da.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $v<0$ whenever $0<t<4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347373 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32c0fb55-c598-521f-aab9-72efc6b70789" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Time Intervals on which a Particle is Moving in a Particular Direction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71f50476-7bb0-52b6-a106-0307c753e044" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<t<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347375} {:answer/id #uuid "8e909557-402f-5a89-af4a-a06569e803c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t>2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347376} {:answer/id #uuid "5c96c751-5057-56ea-a9ae-fcc675d8458e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0<t<2$ and $t>2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347377} {:answer/id #uuid "0cc2eadc-65a0-5eb6-88d3-80c1b56fb6d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2<t<3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347378} {:answer/id #uuid "123e61bb-87c5-56e9-93a1-2b831ce6387c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t>1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c96c751-5057-56ea-a9ae-fcc675d8458e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0<t<2$ and $t>2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347377} :answer-field/id #uuid "1deb23e8-243c-5879-a0bc-5abdd02f8eb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "44fe0fb9-e449-5b62-8c42-a1a49cb8c77e" :question/math-academy-id "q-120758" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=3{t}^{2}-8t+4,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  The positive direction is left to right. If the particle is stationary when $t=2\\,\\text{s},$ determine the time intervals in which the particle moves to the right." :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}-8t+4)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}+4t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(2)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(2)^{3}-4(2)^{2}+4(2)+C \\\\ 0 & =8-16+8+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}+4t.$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the particle is moving to the *right*, we must solve the inequality $v>0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}+4t=t(t-2)^{2}>0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t)$, we get the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/assets/9254ea32fc55b2a703e52f55f4292d9708eef63ae147a9f1d2eac40b85eff319.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $v>0$ whenever $0<t<2$ or $t>2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347380 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0a81fe3-c8f1-58ec-ba93-116997b15f1c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Times at Which a Particle Reaches Its Maximum Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afe52287-dbf9-55a5-ad9c-cf9db68b0922" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=37.5\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=5\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347382} {:answer/id #uuid "8a60ca76-c90b-5646-abd4-9991066d6771" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=38\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=5\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347383} {:answer/id #uuid "cc94764a-6f49-5af6-b0c6-8c3d48aea0ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=14\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=1\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347384} {:answer/id #uuid "951b86ca-e7a1-547c-9e08-39cdec444d68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=24.5\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=2\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347385} {:answer/id #uuid "c2cd26d8-2498-5d91-bab0-2d31c640525b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=75\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=5\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a60ca76-c90b-5646-abd4-9991066d6771" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=38\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=5\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347383} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c206aea-ad4e-5055-b51f-e5b9863968ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b21641d-ae30-55b8-ba2d-d86f82ac4d9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-120784" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=15-3t,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=14\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity." :question/worked-solution "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 15-3t & =0 \\\\ t & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $t=5\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(15-3t) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(5)<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=5\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (15-3t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =15t-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(1)=14.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15(1)-\\frac{3}{2}(1)^{2}+C & =14 \\\\ C & =\\frac{3}{2}-1\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{1}{2}+15t-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}.$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{\\text{max}}=v(5)=\\frac{1}{2}+15(5)-\\frac{3}{2}(5)^{2}=38\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347387 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0a81fe3-c8f1-58ec-ba93-116997b15f1c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Times at Which a Particle Reaches Its Maximum Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76e0049b-e827-5e62-910c-cde6073e7393" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=80\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=4\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347389} {:answer/id #uuid "eb5e6ec6-841d-51dd-bf7e-257f6b844688" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=44\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=2\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347390} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ce41a0-d76c-52b5-8dac-80b6d9414ab7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=22\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=1\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347391} {:answer/id #uuid "2e50994c-c2ed-5a2f-84bb-76209c44b40a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=144\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=4\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347392} {:answer/id #uuid "d40cb7a2-d33d-58fa-a3de-7a5d4fcab1e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=64\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=4\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347393}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76e0049b-e827-5e62-910c-cde6073e7393" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=80\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=4\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347389} :answer-field/id #uuid "f833f5a6-2fc9-59e9-8446-89816f02c9f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "abfa67aa-cfad-591f-9920-3a4586b2b99e" :question/math-academy-id "q-120817" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=16t-{t}^{3},$ where $t>0$ is the time  in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=44\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=2\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity." :question/worked-solution "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 16t-{t}^{3} & =0 \\\\ t(16-{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ t(4-t)(4+t) & =0 \\\\ t & =0,\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0$, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{″}(t) & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(16t-{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =16-3{t}^{2} \\\\ {v}^{″}(4) & =16-3(4)^{2} \\\\  & =-32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(4)<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (16t-{t}^{3})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(2)=44.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8(2)^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}(2)^{4}+C & =44 \\\\ 32-4+C & =44\\,⟹\\,C=16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=16+8{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{4}.$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{\\text{max}}=v(4)=16+8(4)^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}(4)^{4}=80\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347394 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c28b400d-9ec3-53c6-8e33-0133598ed143" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Times at Which a Particle is Stationary"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35bf3d2d-740f-5161-8c08-ba736d3aaf1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=4\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347396} {:answer/id #uuid "10ce8023-cdbd-56fa-bf0c-3d08c0dc0889" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347397} {:answer/id #uuid "b44f0bd7-1386-513f-b009-8619f7c87db3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347398} {:answer/id #uuid "e2012a77-0e22-5edb-beca-85ec3a7bbfd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=5\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347399} {:answer/id #uuid "3df54e57-8de2-59c4-a19e-81d1c2084c8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35bf3d2d-740f-5161-8c08-ba736d3aaf1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=4\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347396} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bd36f75-8321-5120-a843-88471e9add0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347395}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "80fca8ce-b95f-5f16-bd48-d2a1b1bfcf3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-120843" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=3{t}^{2}-6t-4,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=30\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=5\\,\\text{s},$ determine the moment in time when the particle is stationary." :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}-6t-4)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-4t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(5)=30.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30 & =(5)^{3}-3(5)^{2}-4(5)+C \\\\ 30 & =125-75-20+C \\\\ 30 & =30+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-4t.$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-4t & =0 \\\\ t({t}^{2}-3t-4) & =0 \\\\ t(t-4)(t+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $t=-1,0,4.$\n\nSince $t>0,$ we discard solutions $t=-1,0.$ \nTherefore, the particle is stationary when $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347401 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c28b400d-9ec3-53c6-8e33-0133598ed143" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Times at Which a Particle is Stationary"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5328a585-92b3-5837-a587-e243d0e71855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{16}{9}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347403} {:answer/id #uuid "6aa95437-4e13-5748-8205-4673ec98d39b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347404} {:answer/id #uuid "5a8a455a-bb46-5f61-ad45-6f4fb1550d38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347405} {:answer/id #uuid "139391db-d583-5c92-8049-d49e4a7df0a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=16\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347406} {:answer/id #uuid "cda47f23-e809-59f7-9623-50d963c13109" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5328a585-92b3-5837-a587-e243d0e71855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{16}{9}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347403} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fd3bd4f-c504-5ecc-bdee-da6e6dcdbde4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a8cd1df7-b5ee-52ff-a440-dc872cbe554f" :question/math-academy-id "q-120847" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=\\sqrt{t}-t,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s},$ determine the moment in time when the particle is stationary." :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\sqrt{t}-t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(1)=\\frac{1}{6}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} & =\\frac{2}{3}(1)^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{6} & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{6} & =\\frac{1}{6}+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}.$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}=0 \\\\ 4{t}^{3/2}-3{t}^{2}=0 \\\\ {t}^{3/2}(4-3{t}^{1/2}) & =0 \\\\ t & =0,\\,\\frac{16}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0,$ the particle is stationary when $t=\\frac{16}{9}\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347408 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c28b400d-9ec3-53c6-8e33-0133598ed143" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Times at Which a Particle is Stationary"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0180f83f-9a51-5d54-82b9-5c40faaaa53c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347410} {:answer/id #uuid "1c0a402f-dd29-5769-a225-d3f7bcc1f3d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0180f83f-9a51-5d54-82b9-5c40faaaa53c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347410} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfee791d-34de-56db-ab0b-52acf7beef16" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1d16b015-b019-5781-ac85-1dc7c79b07e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-349452" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=4\\sqrt{t},$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=\\frac{7}{3}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s},$ determine the moment in time when the particle is stationary.\n\nThe particle is stationary at $t=$\n{{field-1}}$\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (4\\sqrt{t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{t}^{3/2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(1)=\\frac{7}{3}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{3} & =\\frac{8}{3}(1)^{3/2}+C \\\\ \\frac{7}{3} & =\\frac{8}{3}+C \\\\ C & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{8}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{8}{3}{t}^{3/2} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 8{t}^{3/2} & =1 \\\\ {t}^{3/2} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ t & ={(\\frac{1}{8})}^{2/3}=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary when $t=\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347412 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32c0fb55-c598-521f-aab9-72efc6b70789" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Time Intervals on which a Particle is Moving in a Particular Direction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b252b44-084d-547d-94e2-ffe621d06abd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b252b44-084d-547d-94e2-ffe621d06abd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347414} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d76f71f-f6bb-5c3f-8d9a-c88aadd2f3a9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347413} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c435ff07-035e-5ba5-9b5f-e31c437778ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c435ff07-035e-5ba5-9b5f-e31c437778ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347416} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab74e609-b28e-540e-8f5c-1dc5141a8fb4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "74c3dc46-3475-526b-a5f2-58b999ab5666" :question/math-academy-id "q-354843" :question/problem "A runner moves along a straight track relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=3{t}^{2}-2t-12,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. The positive direction is left to right. If the runner is stationary when $t=4\\,\\text{s},$ determine the time interval in which the runner moves to the left.\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$<t<$\n\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}-2t-12)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-{t}^{2}-12t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(4)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(4)^{3}-(4)^{2}-12(4)+C \\\\ 0 & =64-16-48+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the runner at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}-{t}^{2}-12t.$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the runner is moving to the *left*, we must solve the inequality $v<0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}-12t=t(t-4)(t+3)<0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t)$, we get the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/assets/27b9a41b0ad5c3ff98f6d76e24f0dc74e863d8f63a4c033fc41b7f7f2ebd72ea.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $v<0$ whenever $0\\,<\\,t\\,<\\,4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347417 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0a81fe3-c8f1-58ec-ba93-116997b15f1c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Times at Which a Particle Reaches Its Maximum Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4761a1cb-26c1-5010-801f-2629c4a25c87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4761a1cb-26c1-5010-801f-2629c4a25c87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347419} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a6b04ad-eb88-5f33-a2dd-e7fbe24850cf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347418} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "215af426-de46-5442-8fb0-080aa787c3f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347421}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "215af426-de46-5442-8fb0-080aa787c3f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347421} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ebfd94f-bef6-594b-843c-97325e65ce7b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c42ef4dd-57c4-5ec3-b31b-d457d324859f" :question/math-academy-id "q-354997" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=3\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the earliest time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity.\n\nThe maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ is reached first time at $t=$\n{{field-2}}$\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$  \n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =0 \\\\ 4t & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer.  \n\nTherefore, the extrema of the velocity function, for $t>0,$ are\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\frac{3\\pi }{8},\\frac{5\\pi }{8},…\n$$\n\nWe now test the extrema values, starting with the least, using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)) \\\\  & =-48\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8},$ the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =-48\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =-48\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-48\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-48<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}.$  Therefore, the first time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity is $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=3.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+C & =3 \\\\ 0+C & =3\\,⟹\\,C=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nv(t)=3+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{\\text{max}} & =v(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =3+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =3+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =6\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the maximum velocity  ${v}_{\\text{max}}=6\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ is reached first time at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347422 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0a81fe3-c8f1-58ec-ba93-116997b15f1c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Times at Which a Particle Reaches Its Maximum Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6a02a03-3676-5442-8ba9-7ad757ac4388" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347424} {:answer/id #uuid "19613a9d-1f0c-5113-8521-c6f055a0cd94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347425}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6a02a03-3676-5442-8ba9-7ad757ac4388" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347424} :answer-field/id #uuid "b46fda3f-1819-5d2b-b6c5-4bdc7ea4c1da" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347423} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a571561-a287-5a82-9ba5-4038d426bfd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347427}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a571561-a287-5a82-9ba5-4038d426bfd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347427} :answer-field/id #uuid "558d7c46-5562-5e0d-b935-3568a185730a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "756b3b1d-bd10-5418-b2ff-ec0ca51de06d" :question/math-academy-id "q-354998" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t),$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the earliest time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity.\n\nThe maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ is reached first time at $t=$\n{{field-2}}$\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$  \n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & =0 \\\\ 5t & =\\pi n \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\pi }{5}n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer.  \n\nTherefore, the extrema of the velocity function, for $t>0,$ are\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{5},\\frac{2\\pi }{5},\\frac{3\\pi }{5},…\n$$\n\nWe now test the extrema values, starting with the least, using the second derivative test. For this, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\  & =50\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $t=\\frac{\\pi }{5},$ the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{5}) & =50\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5}) \\\\  & =50\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =-50<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{5})<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{5}.$ Therefore, the first time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity is $t=\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+C & =2 \\\\ -2+C & =2 \\\\ C & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nv(t)=4-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{\\text{max}} & =v(\\frac{\\pi }{5}) \\\\  & =4-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5}) \\\\  & =4-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =6\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the maximum velocity  ${v}_{\\text{max}}=6\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ is reached first time at  $t=\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347428 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c28b400d-9ec3-53c6-8e33-0133598ed143" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Times at Which a Particle is Stationary"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aecd7b3c-388a-5bc4-bcd2-b9c65bdea044" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347430} {:answer/id #uuid "fc1b5410-c540-5e94-acc1-7caf2760304e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aecd7b3c-388a-5bc4-bcd2-b9c65bdea044" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347430} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc076205-34f1-5cb9-af02-01bc649cc56f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2782961b-f944-50aa-9da6-831addd71b10" :question/math-academy-id "q-355266" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=\\frac{2}{t},$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t={e}^{3}\\,\\text{s}$, determine the moment in time when the particle is stationary. Do not round your answer.\n\nThe particle is stationary at $t=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v({e}^{3})=4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3})+C \\\\ 4 & =6+C \\\\ C & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)-2.$ \n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)-2 & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(t) & =1 \\\\ t & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary when $t=e\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347432 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0a81fe3-c8f1-58ec-ba93-116997b15f1c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Times at Which a Particle Reaches Its Maximum Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4df4282-2d4f-56bd-af03-103f59a7e12f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=13\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=2\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347434} {:answer/id #uuid "7711b930-01f2-5a95-bace-cc856fca6b1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=7\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=3\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347435} {:answer/id #uuid "82c65a4b-9a32-5b54-98f8-0561623315c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{29}{3}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=1\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347436} {:answer/id #uuid "17e09cd5-7896-5ee4-8a9b-c741a377f458" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{20}{3}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=2\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347437} {:answer/id #uuid "30cb3d5b-a778-5aba-8855-acfc1cbdc187" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=3\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7711b930-01f2-5a95-bace-cc856fca6b1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=7\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=3\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347435} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ed0ae30-1508-5f62-a3cf-94feebd8851a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2cf69b21-8b56-5114-88ce-a8607481a182" :question/math-academy-id "q-49950" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=2-\\frac{2}{3}t,$ where $t⩾0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity." :question/worked-solution "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 2-\\frac{2}{3}t & =0 \\\\ t & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $t=3\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t)$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-\\frac{2}{3}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(3)<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=3\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2-\\frac{2}{3}t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2t-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)-\\frac{1}{3}(0)^{2}+C & =4 \\\\ 0+0+C & =4\\,⟹\\,C=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2t-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{2}+4.$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{\\text{max}}=v(3)=2(3)-\\frac{1}{3}(3)^{2}+4=7\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347439 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32c0fb55-c598-521f-aab9-72efc6b70789" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Time Intervals on which a Particle is Moving in a Particular Direction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5128612f-11f2-5dc4-9998-34d5b6a66ae5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<t<3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347441} {:answer/id #uuid "8ccd4b04-28a5-5463-91b9-66b8e84b1ead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t>3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347442} {:answer/id #uuid "09c0363f-c3b5-5117-b1d6-72d825e9221f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t>6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347443} {:answer/id #uuid "17929e29-9f1e-5684-adc5-4233adc45a3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2<t<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347444} {:answer/id #uuid "cd908dd7-34ff-537b-ac9c-19ae6af7d058" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3<t<6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347445}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5128612f-11f2-5dc4-9998-34d5b6a66ae5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<t<3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347441} :answer-field/id #uuid "966c0670-6430-59ff-96bc-a2d570314685" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c6b97c2d-852b-5777-8eb7-01af2dbc5e9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-81769" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=1-2t$, where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. The positive direction is left to right. If the particle has velocity $v=-6\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=4\\,\\text{s}$, determine the time intervals in which the particle moves to the right." :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (1-2t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t-{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(4)=-6.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6 & =(4)-(4)^{2}+C \\\\ -6 & =4-16+C \\\\ -6 & =-12+C \\\\ C & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=-{t}^{2}+t+6.$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the particle is moving to the *right*, we must solve the inequality $v>0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n-{t}^{2}+t+6=-({t}^{2}-t-6)=-(t+2)(t-3)>0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t),$ we get the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/assets/74db16dc00f5d08637cef02960409b7fd15c4bd3c19f577fa5a1f30ac8c402d3.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $v>0$ whenever $0<t<3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c28b400d-9ec3-53c6-8e33-0133598ed143" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Times at Which a Particle is Stationary"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7af66cda-aaef-5313-9d8d-9de23732d953" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347448} {:answer/id #uuid "366ce089-af0b-576a-91ad-6e7bb46cea29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=4\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347449} {:answer/id #uuid "7dfcaaa2-706d-5451-b21e-e082976764ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347450} {:answer/id #uuid "82948b0b-b805-5795-8584-49f23e5576fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=6\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347451} {:answer/id #uuid "171b0ebe-cd84-5a1e-b75d-6daa6a5860e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "366ce089-af0b-576a-91ad-6e7bb46cea29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=4\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347449} :answer-field/id #uuid "d96a4a06-b908-5652-b52a-7e65409395f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ae25c3d-ae39-50a4-b8c4-43bcaa22b063" :question/math-academy-id "q-84233" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=1\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=9\\,\\text{s},$ determine the moment in time when the particle is stationary." :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(9)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\sqrt{9}+C \\\\ 1 & =3+C \\\\ C & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\sqrt{t}-2.$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{t}-2 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{t} & =2 \\\\ t & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary when $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$"}]}
